  "配文“你好大峡谷”。逆光剪影：他侧身站在一块伸出崖边的岩石顶上眺望峡谷，拍的人在后方低处，人和岩石在画面左边成剪影，右边大片是峡谷，太阳在左上角。": "Captioned “Hello, Grand Canyon.” It's a backlit silhouette: he stands side-on atop a rock that juts out from the rim, gazing over the canyon. The photographer is behind him and lower down. He and the rock form a silhouette on the left, the canyon fills most of the right side, and the sun sits in the upper left corner.",
  "Mather Point、Yavapai Point、Hopi Point 的观景台都能找到“前景是岩石、后面是峡谷”的角度。上午 9 点前或下午 5 点后太阳低，剪影最好看；拍的人退远用长焦，护栏里侧的人也能拍出站在边上的感觉。": "The overlooks at Mather Point, Yavapai Point, and Hopi Point all have angles with rock in the foreground and canyon behind. Silhouettes look best before 9 a.m. or after 5 p.m., when the sun is low. If the photographer steps well back and uses a telephoto lens, someone standing behind the railing can still look like they're right at the edge.",
  "他站的岩石外面没有护栏，不要照搬：大峡谷几乎每年都有人在崖边拍照坠落。人离崖边至少 2 米、站在护栏里侧，靠角度和长焦营造效果。": "There's no railing beyond the rock he's standing on, so don't copy this shot exactly. Almost every year someone falls at the Grand Canyon while taking photos near the edge. Stay behind the railing and at least 6 feet (2 m) from the edge, and get the effect with the angle and a telephoto lens.",
  "刮大风、雨雪后岩石湿滑时，不要靠近任何没有护栏的边缘。": "In strong wind, or when the rock is slick after rain or snow, stay away from any unrailed edge.",
